Implementation notes:
A Str is a Fearless string literal: #empty is "", #path is "path", #kind is "kind", #mounts is "mounts" and #attenuators is "attenuators".
Each Message of fearlessManager.queue and of fearlessManager.longLivedMessages keeps the connection that the Message arrived on; a Message that fearlessManager$delegate returns keeps the connection of the Message it comes from; a Terminate Message that a Quit adds to fearlessManager.queue keeps the connection of that Quit; a Terminate Message that a "RefreshCache" adds to fearlessManager.queue keeps the connection of that "RefreshCache"; a "RefreshCache" that fearlessManager adds to fearlessManager.queue keeps the connection of the Message that fearlessManager refuses; fearlessManager answers a Message on that connection.
An implementation can find os$folderTimeChangesAt(TaggedText) in any way that gives the right answer for the file system of TaggedText. An implementation could find it by doing it: note the modification time of a folder, wait until the clock of that file system is after that time, add a file to that folder, and read the modification time again.
The 'manager path' is chosen by the implementation, and can differ between operating systems: each version of fearlessManager has its own ManagerFolder, and a copy of fearlessManager at another path has its own ManagerFolder.
The lock of a LockFile can be any lock of the desk with two properties: the OS releases the lock when the process ends, however the process ends, and the lock follows the file when the file is renamed.
Finding that another process holds the lock, and taking the lock, are one step. An implementation could find it by trying to take the lock.
The ManagerFolder appears at the 'manager path' complete, in one step, and that step fails when a folder is at the 'manager path' already. An implementation could fill a new folder next to the 'manager path', then rename that new folder to the 'manager path'.
no_manager.lock appears with its lock already taken, in one step, and that step fails when no_manager.lock exists already: a file that is created first and locked after can be deleted by another fearlessManager in between. An implementation could create a file with another name, take the lock of that file, then rename that file to no_manager.lock.
fearlessManager deletes a file with a name of form LockFile only while holding the lock of that file: on Linux a delete ignores the locks of other processes.
Only a fearlessManager renames a LockFile: a process that holds the lock of no_manager.lock can be any program, while a process that holds the lock of candidate_manager.lock is a fearlessManager that is starting.
The Port is in the name of the LockFile, not in the content: on Windows a process can not read a file that another process holds the lock of.
